---
import { href } from "../lib/base.ts";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { STATUSES, STATUS_INFO, plural } from "../lib/mbmap.ts";
import { rawUrl, registry, REPO_URL } from "../lib/registry.ts";
import { LINKS, MODBENCH_URL, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site.ts";

const { devices, manufacturers, statusCounts } = registry();
const example = `{
  "mbmap": 2,
  "vendor": "Example Corp",
  "model": "EM-100",
  "protocol": "rtu",
  "baudrate": 9600,
  "parity": "E",
  "word_order": "big",
  "registers": [
    {"name": "Voltage", "group": "Measurements", "area": "input", "address": 0, "type": "float32", "unit": "V"},
    {"name": "Active energy", "group": "Energy", "area": "holding", "address": 256, "type": "uint32", "scale": 0.01, "unit": "kWh", "word_order": "little"},
    {"name": "Reset energy", "group": "Commands", "area": "coil", "address": 0, "type": "bool", "access": "W"}
  ]
}`;
---

<Base
  title="About MBMap"
  description="MBMap is an open, community-driven registry of Modbus device maps in one JSON format, readable by any tool. What it is, how the format works and how to contribute."
  nav="about"
>
  <div class="about">
    <section class="card pad prose">
      <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        {SITE_NAME} is an open format and a shared, community-maintained registry of Modbus device maps:
        {plural(devices.length, "device")} from {plural(manufacturers.length, "manufacturer")} today, all in the public domain.
      </p>

      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Reading a Modbus device starts with its register map, and that map usually lives in a PDF manual: a table of
        addresses, data types, scale factors and byte orders that every engineer types into their own tool by hand. Each
        tool stores it in its own format, so the same work is repeated for every device, in every tool, by everyone who
        uses it. Mistakes in the copy (an address off by one, a swapped word order) show up as wrong values on site.
      </p>

      <h2>What {SITE_NAME} does</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>.mbmap.json</code> file describes one device model: who makes it, how to connect to it and what every
        register means. Write the map once, check it, and any compatible tool can load it with the variables already named,
        scaled and typed. The registry collects these maps in one place, on
        <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>, where anyone can add a device or correct one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="format">How the format works</h2>
      <p>
        A map is a plain JSON document with a <a href={LINKS.schema}>JSON Schema</a>. Top-level keys give the manufacturer,
        the model and the connection defaults (protocol, unit id, serial settings, byte and word order, address base).
        Each register has a name, a Modbus area (<code>coil</code>, <code>discrete</code>, <code>input</code>,
        <code>holding</code>), an address and a type, and may add a unit, a scale, access rights, write limits, translation
        tables for states and bit fields. One register per line keeps every change a one-line diff.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The full rules, including validation codes every reader reports the same way, are in the
        <a href={LINKS.spec}>specification (SPEC.md)</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="status">How far a map can be trusted</h2>
      <p>
        Every map in the library says where it comes from in <code>x_status</code>. It is a library convention, not part of
        the format, and Modbench shows it next to each map.
      </p>
      <ul class="status-list">
        {
          STATUSES.map((s) => (
            <li>
              <span class={`status ${s}`}>
                <Icon name={s} /> {STATUS_INFO[s].label}
              </span>
              <span>
                {STATUS_INFO[s].long} <span class="faint">({statusCounts[s]} maps)</span>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        Many unofficial maps were converted from other open catalogues to get the library started, and nobody has checked them
        against the manufacturer's documentation yet. Whatever the status, the maps are provided as is: check a map against
        the manufacturer's documentation before you write to a live device.
      </p>

      <h2 id="modbench">{SITE_NAME} and Modbench</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={MODBENCH_URL}>Modbench</a> is a Modbus TCP/RTU client for Windows, Linux and macOS, and the main tool
        that reads {SITE_NAME} maps. It browses and downloads this library directly, reads and writes a device by register
        name, checks the device against the map's models and firmware, and can run a simulated device shaped after a map.
        This website is its companion on the web: the same maps and the same look, for finding a device before you connect to it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="integrate">Using the maps in your own tool</h2>
      <p>The format is open and any tool can read it. Everything is served from the repository:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={rawUrl("index.json")}><code>index.json</code></a> lists every map with its path, size, SHA-256, status and register count.</li>
        <li>Each map is at <code>{rawUrl("maps/<vendor>/<model>.mbmap.json")}</code>.</li>
        <li>
          A signed copy of the index is published on the <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/signed`}><code>signed</code></a> branch,
          so an app can check that the maps it downloads are the library's (see <a href={`${LINKS.contributing}#signed-index`}>Signed index</a>).
        </li>
        <li>The <a href={LINKS.tests}><code>tests/</code></a> folder has sample files and the diagnostics a reader must report, to check an implementation against the spec.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Support part of the format or all of it, then open a pull request to list your tool on the
        <a href={href("/compatibility")}>compatibility page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>An open community project</h2>
      <p>
        The maps, the specification and the tools are dedicated to the public domain under <a href={LINKS.license}>CC0 1.0</a>:
        use them for anything, including commercial products, without asking. The registry grows with contributions from
        engineers, technicians, integrators and manufacturers: new maps, corrections and tests on real hardware.
        <a href={href("/contribute")}>Here is how to help</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="card pad links" aria-label="Links">
      <h2 class="key">Links</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={REPO_URL}><Icon name="github" /> mbmap-library on GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href={LINKS.spec}><Icon name="library" /> Specification (SPEC.md)</a></li>
        <li><a href={LINKS.schema}><Icon name="raw" /> JSON Schema</a></li>
        <li><a href={LINKS.contributing}><Icon name="plus" /> CONTRIBUTING.md</a></li>
        <li><a href={LINKS.example}><Icon name="official" /> Example map</a></li>
        <li><a href={LINKS.discussions}><Icon name="about" /> Discussions</a></li>
        <li><a href={MODBENCH_URL}><Icon name="registers" /> Modbench</a></li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 260px;
    gap: 16px;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .about h1 {
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }

  .about .lead {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .status-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0 !important;
  }

  .status-list li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 110px 1fr;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .links {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--bar-h) + 16px);
  }

  .links ul {
    margin: 8px -8px 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .links a {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 9px;
    padding: 7px 8px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .links a:hover {
    background: var(--accent-soft);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .links {
      position: static;
    }
    .status-list li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
